# JUSTIN — "No Audience" — expansion chapters + two endings
# Continues story/14_justin.s25. Flags/vars created here are prefixed justinx_.
# Chain: justin_ch1 → … → justin_ch6 → act4_choice → justin_final → (justin_ending | _beside | _someday)

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH1: THE LEDGER
=== justin_ch1
@autosave
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade
@music studio
@amb room
@time 03:10
@fx dust n=0.3
@cam wide
> Every route through this building passes the front desk. That was the whole idea of the front desk.
> Justin has walked past it all night. This time he stops, and puts both elbows on the counter, and leans over to look at the side customers never see.
@show justin thinking at=c
justin: What's it like back there?
you: Smaller than it looks. There's a heater under the desk that only warms your left foot.
justin[smile]: Only the left?
you: The right one learns to cope.
> The counter flap sticks. It always did. You lift, then push, and it swings up the way it swung up a hundred nights one winter.
> Your hands knew how before you'd decided to try.
justin[smile]: You didn't even look at it. I know what that is. That's a routine.
justin[thinking]: Is there a book? There was a book. I had to write my name in a book.
> Second drawer from the bottom. It needs a hip, not a hand. You give it the hip.
@sfx door
> Inside: a stapler, a dead torch, a ring of keys with a cracked plastic 25 on the tag — and a green hardback ledger, soft at the corners.
> ROOM HIRE, it says on the cover, in marker. Underneath, in a different marker: *please write clearly. please.*
justin: That's it. That's the book. I'm in there somewhere.
justin[smile]: February. Three years ago. I want to see what I wrote. I was so nervous I think I spelled my own name wrong.
* [playful] "Easy. We look for the worst handwriting."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[laugh]: Rude. Accurate. My sevens look like ones and my ones look like accidents.
    > He flips pages fast, the way he does everything, and overshoots February twice.
* [sincere] "Tell me the date. I'll find the night."
    @rel justin trust+1
    justin[thinking]: The ninth. No — the tenth, technically. It was after midnight.
    > You turn the pages for him. Slowly. He watches your hands instead of the book.
* [quiet] Turn straight to the page with the coffee ring.
    @rel justin trust+1 closeness+1
    > February had a coffee ring on it. You put it there. You remember being annoyed at yourself for a week.
    justin[thinking]: You knew exactly where that was.
    you: I spilled on it. I felt terrible. It's a very easy month to find.
@sfx page
@cam closeup justin
> And there it is. One line among a lot of lines.
> ROOM B. IN: 00:30. NAME: a J, and then a small, panicked scribble.
> PURPOSE: *dance*, crossed out. *practice*, crossed out. And then, pressed hard enough to dent the paper: *nothing*.
justin[neutral]: …I wrote "nothing."
justin: I remember that. I didn't want anybody to read "dance" and come and look.
justin[smile]: Great plan. Flawless. Nobody came to look. Except the one person with the keys.
> He runs a finger along the line to the next column, and stops.
> OUT: 01:00. It isn't his handwriting. It's rounder. It leans the other way.
> It's yours.
justin[thinking]: One o'clock. But I wasn't out at one. You stood in the door at — what, ten to? And then I did the bad eight, and then I packed up *so* slowly—
justin: It was twenty past. At least. I looked at the clock in the lobby and felt sick.
> There's a laminated card still taped to the desk. LATE FINISH: 1,500 WON PER TEN MINUTES. Someone has drawn a small sad face next to the number.
justin[smile]: So I've owed this building three thousand won for three years. That's not a record I wanted.
> He's already reaching into his hoodie pocket. He comes up with a folded five-thousand note and looks around for somewhere official to put it.
> The honesty tin is where it always was, behind the stapler. It rattles with exactly one coin.
* [sincere] "I wrote one o'clock on purpose. You looked like you needed the twenty minutes."
    @rel justin trust+1 romance+1
    @flag justinx_twenty
    > He stops with the note halfway to the tin.
    justin[neutral]: …You gave me twenty minutes.
    you: They weren't mine to charge for. Nobody else wanted the room.
    justin[thinking]: I thought I got away with it. I didn't get away with anything. Somebody just let me.
* [playful] "With three years of interest, that's one strawberry milk. Exactly."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[smile]: One strawberry milk. I can't pay that. I have a complicated history with strawberry milk.
    you: I've heard.
    justin[smile]: Everyone's heard. Hyunjun makes sure of it.
* [quiet] Put the lid back on the tin. "Desk's closed."
    @rel justin trust+1
    > He looks at the tin. At the note. At you.
    justin[smile]: You can't just close a desk at me.
    you: I did it every night for a whole winter. I'm very good at it.
> He puts the note in the tin anyway. All five thousand. He presses the lid down with both thumbs, like he's sealing a letter.
justin: Keep the change, Studio 25.
> Then he goes back to the ledger, because there's one more column, and he's only just seen it.
> REMARKS. Most nights it's empty. Some nights it says *heater broken again* or *who keeps taking the pens*.
> On his line, in your round, leaning handwriting, it says: *Room B — good weird.*
@expr justin neutral
> He reads it twice. You can tell, because his eyes go back to the start of the line.
justin: "Good weird."
justin[thinking]: That's what you said in the doorway. I asked if it was weird, with no music. You said, "A little. Good weird. Keep going."
justin: And then you came down here and wrote it in the *book*.
you: The column was empty. It seemed like it should say something.
> He doesn't laugh. He puts his hand flat on the page, next to the line, not covering it.
justin[neutral]: I said earlier I never thanked you properly. For staying up.
> His shoulders start to shape a bow. A big one. Theatrical, one arm out. You watch him feel it coming.
> He lets it go. He just stands there, on the customer side of the counter, with his hands at his sides.
justin: Thank you for staying up.
justin: Thank you for the twenty minutes. I didn't know there were twenty minutes.
justin[shy]: And for "good weird." I'd have danced for another year on "good weird," if I'd known it was written down somewhere.
* [sincere] "You're welcome. Properly."
    @rel justin trust+1
    justin[smile]: Properly. Yeah. That's how it's supposed to go. One person says it, the other person takes it.
    justin[smile]: I always skip the second half. I say thanks and I'm already out the door.
* [sincere] "It was the best shift I worked all winter. I never told anyone that either."
    @rel justin trust+1 romance+1
    justin[thinking]: The best one. I saluted you and lost my footing.
    you: Before that. The part where you didn't know I was there.
    > He looks down at the page, then back up at you, and holds the look. He doesn't reach for a joke to cover it.
* [playful] "Would you like a receipt?"
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[smile]: Yes. Absolutely. I want it in writing.
    > You take the pen on its string and write, under his line: *PAID IN FULL. Three years late. Dancer was polite about it.*
    justin[smile]: "Dancer." Not "debtor." I'll take it.
@flag justinx_thanked
@cam wide
> The key ring is still in the drawer. He hooks it out with one finger and holds it up between you. It turns slowly, catching the desk lamp.
justin[thinking]: These are them. The ones in the mirror.
> He sets them in your palm instead of handing them over. The weight is exactly what your hand expected.
> A cracked 25 in white paint. It used to knock against your leg on every stair.
justin[smile]: Somebody has to lock up. Tonight it's you again.
you: There's nothing to lock. The power goes at five.
justin: I know. Carry them anyway. It looks right.
> You put them in your coat pocket. They knock once as you come back through the flap, and both of you hear it.
@flag justinx_keys
@goto justin_ch2

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH2: THE TRIAL (light)
=== justin_ch2
@autosave
@bg studio_lounge t=fade
@music fun
@amb room
@time 03:26
@cam wide
> The lounge has one working thing in it, and it's the vending machine. It hums in the corner like it's proud of this.
> Row three, second from the left: a single carton of strawberry milk. The last in the building.
@show justin thinking at=c
justin: I'm paying debts tonight. I'm on a roll. Hyunjun gets his milk back.
> He pats his hoodie pocket. Then the other one. Then, slowly, the pockets of his joggers.
justin[neutral]: …I put all my money in a tin.
you: All five thousand.
justin[smile]: I said "keep the change." It was a good line. I stand by it.
> The machine wants 1,100 won, coins only. A handwritten sign on the glass says NOTES MAKE IT SAD.
> You turn out your coat pockets onto the low table. A five-hundred, three hundreds, some lint, the keys.
justin: Eight hundred. We're three hundred short. That's solvable.
> He's already lifting the cushions off the sofa, one at a time, and stacking them on the armrest.
* [playful] Take the other end of the sofa. Race him.
    @rel justin closeness+1
    > You find a hundred-won coin and a hair tie. He finds a guitar pick, a pen with Songha's name on it, and a token for the claw machine.
    justin[smile]: A hundred. You win. I got stationery, and a token Daniel would pay real money for.
* [curious] "What's the most you've ever found in a sofa?"
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[thinking]: A whole phone once. It was mine. I'd been looking for it for two days.
    > He surfaces with a hundred-won coin held up between two fingers, plus a guitar pick and a claw machine token.
    justin[smile]: And today: riches.
* [quiet] Hold the cushions so he can dig with both hands.
    @rel justin trust+1
    > He goes in up to the elbow. Out comes a guitar pick, a claw machine token, and, finally, a hundred-won coin.
    justin[smile]: Teamwork. You're the muscle. I'm the arm.
> Nine hundred. He looks at the machine. The machine hums.
@show haruta smile at=l
haruta: Good morning. Is it morning? I heard furniture.
justin: Haruta. Do you have two hundred won and no questions?
> Haruta considers this with real care, takes out a small coin purse, and counts two hundred won into Justin's hand.
haruta: I have one question.
justin[smile]: I said *no* questions.
haruta[thinking]: Is it for something Hyunjun is going to be upset about?
justin: It's for something Hyunjun is going to be *happy* about. For once. Write the date down.
> The coins go in. He presses the button for row three, second from the left, with great ceremony.
@sfx vending
> The machine thinks about it. Something turns. Something drops.
@sfx can
> It's yellow.
justin[neutral]: That's banana.
haruta: That is banana.
> In the window, the last strawberry milk hasn't moved. It sits there, pink and untroubled.
justin: I pressed strawberry. {{playerName}} will confirm.
you: You pressed strawberry. With a flourish.
justin[smile]: Then the flourish is what did it. …No. It's a machine. It's just wrong.
@show hyunjun thinking at=r
hyunjun: I heard a vending machine. At three-thirty. Standing next to Justin.
> Hyunjun has arrived in the doorway with his bag over one shoulder and the calm of a detective in the last ten minutes of the film.
hyunjun: Nobody move. Haruta, you're the judge.
haruta[thinking]: I am?
hyunjun: You're the only one here who's never taken anything from my bag.
haruta[smile]: That's true. …All right. Court is open. Please sit.
> Everyone sits. Haruta takes the armchair. It suits him. Justin holds the banana milk in front of him in both hands, like evidence, or an offering.
justin: Your honour. I'd like to make restitution. This is milk. It's the wrong milk. But it's *bought*. With money. By me.
hyunjun: With whose money?
justin[smile]: …It was a group effort.
hyunjun: {{playerName}}. You were with him. You're the witness.
* [playful] "Your honour, I drank half the evidence an hour ago."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    hyunjun[thinking]: Half.
    justin[smile]: I did say it's better if we're both guilty. I've had this defence ready for hours.
    haruta: The witness is now also the accused. This is getting complicated. I like it.
* [sincere] "He's been paying things back all night. This is him trying."
    @rel justin trust+1
    > Hyunjun looks at Justin. Justin looks at the carton.
    hyunjun[thinking]: …Paying things back. Him.
    justin: I put five thousand won in a tin. For a late fee. From three years ago.
    hyunjun[smile]: Three years late, and he still paid it. All right. I respect that.
* [quiet] Take the straw off the carton and hold it out to Hyunjun.
    @rel justin trust+1
    > Hyunjun takes it. Turns it over. It's a very small peace treaty, and he knows it.
    hyunjun[smile]: The witness is more persuasive than the lawyer.
    justin[laugh]: The witness is the only reason I have a lawyer.
haruta: I have reached a verdict.
haruta[smile]: Banana is accepted. Justin carries Hyunjun's bag to breakfast. And he has to say "excuse me" before he opens it, from now on.
justin: "Excuse me"? To a *bag*?
hyunjun[laugh]: To the bag. Every time. I'll be listening.
> Hyunjun pushes the straw in and drinks. He makes a face like it's fine, which from him is a rave.
> Then, to you, not quite quietly enough:
hyunjun: You know I leave it open, right? The bag.
justin[neutral]: …What?
hyunjun: The zip. I leave it open. Since, like, last spring.
hyunjun[thinking]: When he's fine, he takes my stuff and makes a whole thing out of it. When he's not, he buys his own and goes and sits by the window.
hyunjun: So I leave it open. If something's missing, it's a good day.
> Justin doesn't say anything. The banana milk has gone, so he has nothing to hold. He sets his hands on his knees and leaves them there.
justin[neutral]: I thought I was really good at it.
hyunjun[smile]: You're terrible at it. You hum.
haruta: He does hum. It's usually in four.
> Justin laughs, but late, and it comes out smaller than his usual one. He's looking at the zip of Hyunjun's bag like it's a sentence he misread for a year.
* [sincere] "You went quiet. Do you want a minute, or company?"
    @rel justin trust+1 romance+1
    justin[thinking]: …Company.
    justin[smile]: I didn't have to think about that.
    > You stay where you are, next to him, and let the other two talk about breakfast over your heads for a while.
* [playful] "So the great thief had an accomplice the whole time."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[smile]: An inside man. In his own bag.
    hyunjun: I'm not an accomplice. I'm a sponsor.
    justin[smile]: …A sponsor. That's generous of you, actually.
* [quiet] Nudge his sneaker with your foot. Once.
    @rel justin closeness+1 trust+1
    > He nudges back. Then again, a beat later. Then it has a rhythm, and neither of you mentions it.
justin[neutral]: Hyunjun.
hyunjun: Mm.
justin: Thanks. For the zip.
> It's the second proper thank-you he's said tonight. It comes easier than the first. You watch him notice that too.
hyunjun[neutral]: Don't make it a speech. It's a zip.
hyunjun[smile]: …You're welcome. Excuse me.
justin[smile]: You don't have to say it to *me*—
hyunjun: I'm practising. For the bag.
@hide hyunjun
@hide haruta
> Court adjourns. Haruta goes with Hyunjun to find Hamin's biscuits, counting something under his breath as he goes. *Ichi, ni.* Politely.
> Justin stays on the sofa with the claw machine token, walking it across his knuckles and back.
justin[thinking]: He left it open. For a year. And I thought nobody could tell the difference between my days.
justin[smile]: Turns out I've got an audience for the quiet ones too. They've just been really polite about it.
> He flips the token to you. You catch it, which surprises both of you.
justin[smile]: Keep it. Daniel has a standing appointment with that claw.
@flag justinx_zip
@goto justin_ch3

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH3: FIVE STRINGS (quiet)
=== justin_ch3
@autosave
@bg studio_practice t=fade
@music quiet
@amb room
@time 03:44
@fx dust n=0.4
@cam wide
> Practice Room B has a cupboard you'd forgotten about. Grey metal, waist-high, under the window.
@video vid_justin_drama
@unlock cg:cg_justin_drama
> You used to keep the spare tape in it, and the mop, and one thing that was never signed out the whole winter.
> The smallest key on the ring still fits. It turns with a click you feel in your wrist before you hear it.
@sfx click
@show justin thinking at=c
justin: There's a guitar in there?
> There is. A studio acoustic with a paper tag tied to the neck: ASK AT DESK. The top string is gone. The other five are hanging on out of habit.
you: Nobody ever asked at the desk.
justin[smile]: I'm asking at the desk.
you: Granted.
> He lifts it out with both hands, the way you'd pick up something asleep. Sits cross-legged on the floor with his back to the mirror. Finds the guitar pick from the sofa in his pocket and looks at it like the night is showing off.
> The first strum is awful. He winces, and lets it ring out anyway.
justin[smile]: Okay. Three years in a cupboard. Fair.
justin[thinking]: I don't have a tuner. My phone's at six percent and Hanbi has the only charger in Seoul.
justin: Can you hum? One note. Any note. Just hold it still so I've got something to stand on.
* [sincere] Hum the first note that comes. Hold it as steady as you can.
    @rel justin trust+1
    > It wavers, then settles. He turns a peg, slowly, until the string rises and meets you. When it does, the two sounds stop being two.
    justin[smile]: There. Don't move. That's the floor. Everything else gets built on that.
* [playful] "I only hum off-key. It's a condition of my contract."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    justin[laugh]: Off-key is fine. It just has to be the *same* off-key for ten seconds.
    > You manage eight. He tunes the string to your wrong note without a word of complaint, and then tunes everything else to match it.
    justin[smile]: Now the whole guitar is wrong in your direction. That's called a key.
* [quiet] Press a finger to the string where he points, and let him find the note himself.
    @rel justin trust+1 closeness+1
    > He guides your fingertip to the fifth fret. "There. Just hold." He plucks the string beside it, listens, turns, listens.
    > You can feel both strings through the wood. When they agree, the buzz under your finger smooths out.
    justin[smile]: Feel that? That's them agreeing. You don't even need ears.
> It takes a while. He doesn't hurry it. You notice that he doesn't hurry it.
> String by string, the guitar comes back to being a guitar. Five-sixths of one.
@cam closeup justin
justin[thinking]: The slow piece. For November. I couldn't find a song for it.
justin: Every slow song already knows how you're supposed to feel. It tells you. Sad here. Big here. I didn't want to be told.
justin[neutral]: So I've been making one. It's not good. It's eight bars. It stops.
@if var.music == "lyrics"
    justin: There's no words. Sorry. I know you like the ones you can shout along to.
    you: I'll make some up. Badly.
    justin[smile]: Please don't. Please do. Later.
@elif var.music == "instrumental"
    justin: There's no words. You said that's how you like it. Room for your own.
    justin[smile]: I thought about that when I was writing the gaps. I kept leaving more of them.
@elif var.music == "silence"
    justin: It's mostly gaps, honestly. More quiet than notes. You said you like quiet.
    justin[smile]: So you might be the only person this was ever going to work on.
@else
    justin: There's no words. Just the counts, and whatever this turns out to be.
> He plays it.
> It's simple. A low note, held. Three going up, like stairs taken one at a time. A pause long enough to turn around in.
> Then the three again, higher, reaching for the note that would finish it.
> The note that would finish it lives on the missing string. His hand goes to where it should be and finds air.
> The phrase hangs there, unanswered, and he lets it.
@wait 1.2
justin[neutral]: That's where it stops. Every time. Even on a guitar with six strings, that's where I stop.
> He doesn't look up. He's watching his own left hand on the neck, the way he watches his feet.
justin: Can I say a thing? It's not a bit. It might sound like the start of one.
you: Say it.
justin[thinking]: In the team, everyone's got a job. Songha's the plan. Hanbi's the quiet one who's holding everything. Daniel's the engine.
justin: I'm the bright one. That's my spot. When people talk about us, that's the word next to my name.
justin[neutral]: And I like it. I'm not pretending. It's real.
> He plays the low note again. Just that. Lets it ring until it's gone.
justin: But we're going to debut. Someday. Maybe soon. And when we do, whatever I am that day is what I am on camera. For years.
justin[neutral]: I'm scared the spot only fits the loud one. That if I turn up like this — counting, slow, kind of boring — there's no chair for him.
justin: I'm scared. That's the whole sentence. I've never said it without a joke after.
> He waits. You can see him waiting for his own punchline, out of habit. It doesn't come. He nods, once, as if that settles something.
* [sincere] "I met the quiet one first. I stayed four minutes for him. Nobody asked me to."
    @rel justin trust+1 romance+1
    justin[thinking]: …You did meet him first.
    you: The loud one turned up at the end and fell over. I liked him too. But he wasn't why I stayed.
    > He rests his arm along the top of the guitar and looks at you, steadily, for longer than he usually lets himself.
    justin[smile]: I'm keeping that. It goes in the cupboard, with a tag on it.
* [sincere] "It isn't one chair. The person who counted alone for three years is why the loud one lands every turn."
    @rel justin trust+2
    justin[thinking]: …They're the same guy.
    you: You're the only one who thinks there are two.
    justin[smile]: Me and the mirrors. The mirrors are *sure* there's two.
* [quiet] "Play it again."
    @rel justin trust+1 closeness+1
    > He does. Slower. It stops in the same place.
    > You don't tell him it's good. You just stay for the whole thing, the way you stayed once before, and he seems to hear what that means.
    justin[smile]: …You do that. You just stay through the end of things. It's very loud, for something so quiet.
> The strip light at the far end flickers, steadies.
justin[thinking]: I still don't know how it finishes.
> His hand goes up the neck again, to the place where the last note would be. Air.
justin: Hum it? Whatever you think comes next. You were the floor. You might as well be the roof.
* [sincere] Hum the note going up. The one the stairs were climbing toward.
    @rel justin closeness+1
    @flag justinx_hum
    > It's higher than you'd normally dare. It arrives anyway.
    > He plays the three notes under it and stops exactly where you begin, and for a second the phrase is whole.
    justin[smile]: It resolves. Huh. I thought it was a sad one. It's not a sad one.
* [quiet] Hum the first note again. The low one. Bring it home.
    @rel justin trust+1
    @flag justinx_hum
    > The phrase climbs, reaches, and your voice meets it from underneath, back where it started.
    justin[thinking]: It goes home. It goes up the stairs and then it goes home.
    justin[smile]: I've been trying to get it to arrive somewhere new. It just wanted to come back.
* [playful] "Leave it open. Let November finish it."
    @rel justin closeness+1 trust+1
    justin[smile]: A cliffhanger. In my own song.
    justin[thinking]: …No, I like it. It's honest. I'm not finished. Why should it be.
    > He plays the three notes one more time and lets the last one hang, on purpose now. It sounds different when it's chosen.
> He rests his chin on the body of the guitar. His fringe has fallen in his eyes. He doesn't flick it back.
justin[smile]: Nobody's heard that. Not the guys. Not my teacher. It's only ever been played to mirrors.
justin: The mirrors never said anything. You're better than the mirrors.
you: That's the nicest thing anyone's said to me tonight.
justin[laugh]: It's a low bar. They're very dusty.
> He puts the guitar back in the cupboard himself. He leaves the pick tucked under the strings, and turns the paper tag over.
> On the blank side, with the pen from your pocket, he writes: *in tune. mostly. — J*
> You lock the cupboard. The small key turns the same as ever.
@sfx click
@goto justin_ch4

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH4: TEN MINUTES OF NOTHING (he needs something)
=== justin_ch4
@autosave
@bg studio_rooftop t=fade
@music quiet
@amb night
@time 04:02
@fx stars n=0.5
@cam wide
> He says he wants air. You give him a head start and go the long way, past the front desk, where the kettle is one of two things in this building that always worked.
> When you come up onto the roof with two paper cups of barley tea, there are biscuits balanced on the lids.
> You didn't put them there. Hamin was passing the desk. Hamin didn't say a word.
@show justin neutral at=c
> Justin is at the far rail, not leaning on it. Shifting his weight, heel to toe. Rolling one ankle, then the other.
> He's been moving since before midnight. You count back. The pavement, the lobby, the hall, two reconstructions, one slow piece, one bad eight-count, a sofa.
> His legs have a small tremble in them that he's disguising as a groove.
justin[smile]: Tea. You're a good person. I'm just keeping warm. If I stop, I'll cool down, and then I'll feel all of it at once.
you: When did you last sit? Not on a floor between eights. Actually sit.
justin[thinking]: …The sofa. In the lobby. For about a minute. It felt illegal.
> He takes the tea. Drinks it standing, still moving. The biscuit is gone in two bites. He looks surprised to have been hungry.
justin: I'm fine, though. Really. I'm good at tired. Tired's easy. You just keep going and it can't catch you.
* [sincere] "It's caught you. It's standing right here. It brought biscuits."
    @rel justin trust+1
    justin[smile]: Tired brought biscuits?
    you: Hamin brought biscuits. Tired is what he was looking at when he did.
    justin[neutral]: …Oh. He really does notice everything.
* [playful] "Your left knee has been doing a solo for ten minutes."
    @rel justin closeness+1
    > He looks down at it. It's trembling in a neat, quick rhythm.
    justin[smile]: That's a shuffle. It's intentional.
    you: What's it called?
    justin[smile]: …It's called "I should sit down."
* [quiet] Sit down on the bench yourself. Leave room.
    @rel justin trust+1
    > The bench is cold through your coat. You don't say anything. You just hold your tea and look at the space next to you.
    > He lasts about twenty seconds. Then he sits, all at once, the way a man does when he's finally given himself permission.
